{"kind":"request","text":"Design note: intake as capture with a stated source. Write notes/2026-10-21-capture-with-source.md. Planner: manage this to completion. Write the note in a planner worktree once the retained files note (070940da) has landed, or in parallel resting on its current draft; file a checker review; land when approved; then file the builder slices after RF1 to RF4 of retained files.\n\nBackground. Hugh, 2026-10-21, after reading Josh Rosen's \"The Software Factory Debate: Build, Buy, Fork or Delegate\" (x.com/JoshARosen/status/2112902388302467493). Rosen's first job of a software factory is getting work in: trackers, chat, CI failures, incidents, scheduled work. The platforms he names sell intake integrations; dap has none. Issues arrive only by the CLI or MCP, and outside systems run for Git publication only (docs/roadmap.md, \"Outside parties and systems\"). Hugh chose connectors as participants as one of the two levers to act on now (the other, roles and instances for hosted agent teams, is a separate request), and said it intersects with on-boarding from a file or from local work: the matter-room direction of 2026-10-21, in which any person creates a room from their own AI app, \"share this with Dan\" is a recorded act and email is only the channel, and integrations live in the agents, not in dap.\n\nWhat exists (main 603220b9).\n- Three kinds of statement are already distinguished (docs/use-cases.md, \"Three kinds of statement\"): a person's own assertion, an imported document, and another party's signed act.\n- Retained files (request 070940da; draft 2 at e8e0b436 under review): one retained-input kind for files, with digest, size and media type, a keeper port and an upload route. Provenance fields (source, derivation, original name) are left to the application, with a recommended shape to be given. The matter room's capture act is its exit test.\n- The repository import note (notes/2026-10-15-github-repository-import.md): section 6, imported identity does not grant authority; section 7, durable ingress and egress, unknown writes and conflicts, and the rule that an external close is an observation, not proof that local completion conditions passed; delivery stages 1 to 5. Not commissioned.\n- Offline work and propose from a branch (direction of 2026-10-07, design request R5): a participant works offline and proposes from a branch on return. Direction set; not designed.\n- Agent members: kind agent with a person controller; every entry retains member, key and controller. Self-contained application packages install per room (notes/2026-10-15-self-contained-applications.md).\n- A fact from outside enters only as a participant's signed report (roadmap). Evidence rules are landed (d09cc918).\n\nDirection (Hugh, 2026-10-21, with the planner's proposal agreed). One act shape, a capture with a stated source, performed by whichever participant holds the channel. A connector is an agent member, controlled by the person who authorised it, whose acts are captures with provenance. A person's own AI app on-boarding a file or a local branch is the same act, signed by their own agent. GitHub two-way synchronisation is the same agent with an outbox. Propose-from-branch is the code-shaped capture. The lever then collapses to the capture act, retained files, and one reference connector built as a package.\n\nThe note must settle:\n- The capture act: its fields (source kind and address, time captured, the bytes as a file reference or a text, the original name, derivation as fact references, and an external identity such as an issue number or message ID); which item it opens or attaches to; the statement kind as a declared field; and what a guard may conclude from each kind (an imported statement never satisfies a condition that needs a member's own act).\n- The provenance shape that packages agree on, which the retained files note leaves to applications.\n- Who may capture: the roster action; the connector as an agent member with a controller; what the record shows; revocation by retiring the connector's key.\n- Deduplication and ordering: a stable external identity so that a repeated capture of one source is one item; echo suppression for two-way synchronisation (import note, section 7).\n- The authority rule: imported identity grants nothing; an external state change is an observation; the native act applies only when its own guards pass.\n- The on-boarding journeys in order: (1) from a file in a chat session (the matter room, use cases 29 and 30); (2) from a local branch (which part of R5 this note takes and which stays); (3) GitHub issues inbound, as a connector package; (4) an outbox for GitHub comments and closes; (5) Slack and Linear, named and deferred.\n- What the room shows: a captured item carries its source; how the Needs-you and since views treat it.\n- The relation to the checkpoint idea: a capture is the evidence form, and evidence rules consume captures. CI results as captures are a follow-up.\n\nAcceptance. The note states the capture act and its fields, the three statement kinds and what guards may conclude from each, the connector-as-participant model with its authority and revocation, the deduplication and echo rules, the journeys in order with the matter-room file capture as the exit test, and orders the builder slices after retained files. It names the follow-ups: the outbox, Slack, Linear, CI results as captures. Document only; checker review before landing. Style: STE, scene first, terms in order, no registry, decisions or open points in the body (spec writing preferences). Public material: no product names other than GitHub and no comparisons.","body":{"conditions":"Document only; checker review before landing; no comparisons; no product names but GitHub; implementation requests follow after the note lands, after retained files RF1 to RF4.","target":"inherit","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}